==English==

===Alternative forms===
* [[incouragement]] {{qualifier|archaic}}

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|frm|en}} {{term|encoragement|lang=frm}}, from {{etyl|fro|en}} {{term|encoragier|lang=fro}} + {{term|-ment|lang=frm}}, from {{term|en-||make, put in|lang=fro}} + {{term|corage||courage|lang=fro}}, from {{etyl|VL.|en}} {{m|la|*coraticum}}, from {{term|cor||heart, daring|lang=la}} + {{term|-ier|lang=fro}}, suffixed with {{term|-ment|lang=en}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|[ɪnˈkʌrɪdʒmənt]|lang=en}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# The act of encouraging; incitement to action or to practice; as, the encouragement of youth in generosity.
#: ''All generous '''encouragement''' of arts.'' -Otway.
# That which serves to incite, support, promote or advance, as favor, countenance, reward etc.; incentive; increase of confidence; as, the fine arts find little encouragement among a rude people.
#: ''To think of his paternal care, Is a most sweet '''encouragement''' to prayer.'' -Byron.

====Synonyms====
* See also [[Wikisaurus:praise]]

